{"parentUuid":"21ea7cff-d40e-4496-a537-affb8b2a038d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_017xxK4ckh4DNipQRcP4JNBe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're right — I was framing the `/review` rewrite as a \"finding\" when it's actually a **bug to fix**. Let me reframe.\n\n**Direct answer to your follow-up:** I don't have authoritative confirmation, but the structural difference is this:\n\n- **Codex side**: `expandCodexBuiltinReview` rewrites `/review` to paraphrase text *before* spawning. Codex's runtime never sees a slash command. **Confirmed paraphrased, not invoked.**\n- **Claude side**: `buildReviewCmd` does **no** pre-processing — it passes the composed prompt verbatim via `claude -p <prompt>`. So claude receives lines like `/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr` and `/silent-failure-hunter` as the literal first lines of the prompt. Whether claude's `-p` (print/headless) mode resolves those into skill loads the way the interactive TUI does is **the symmetric unknown** — and it's load-bearing for the diagnosis.\n\nI genuinely don't know the answer to \"does `claude -p` resolve slash commands in the prompt.\" There are two plausible answers:\n\n1. **It does** (likely, given the Claude Code runtime is shared between TUI and `-p`). Then claude IS executing the skills while codex IS NOT — that's a real workflow asymmetry that explains a lot of the 2-5x gap.\n2. **It does not** — both agents receive `/...` as opaque prompt text and improvise. Then the asymmetry has to live elsewhere (model defaults, sandbox/MCP cold-start, reasoning effort, tool-loop overhead).\n\nThis is **directly testable** with a 30-second experiment, and it should be the very first thing the diagnostic plan does, because two completely different fix shapes hang off the answer.\n\nRevised Section 1 of the design — let me also restructure to put this verification gate front and center:\n\n## Revised Diagnostic design — Section 1: Phenomenon + The verification gate\n\n**Known facts** (from code audit):\n- `entire review --agent codex` does NOT invoke codex's native `/review`. The string is paraphrased to 28 words of generic instruction before being piped via stdin to `codex exec --json -`. **This is a bug to fix, not just a finding.**\n- `entire review --agent claude-code` passes `/...` skill invocations as the literal first lines of `claude -p <prompt>`. **Unknown whether claude's `-p` mode resolves them as skill loads or treats them as opaque text.**\n- gemini-cli is the same shape as claude (literal prompt via stdin) and uncharacterized.\n- Codex's `DiscoverReviewSkills` is a stub — codex picker only offers curated built-ins + freeform prompt, so codex review is structurally less scripted than claude's regardless of duration.\n\n**The verification gate** (must run before anything else):\n- Run `claude -p \"/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\"` (or any installed Claude plugin skill) in isolation.\n- Observe whether claude actually loads the skill file from `~/.claude/plugins/cache/...` and executes its instructions, vs. treating `/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr` as opaque text.\n- Three ways to tell: (a) JSONL stream (with PR #1192 merged) shows a skill-load tool_use, (b) the output's structure matches the skill's scripted steps, (c) a no-op test: invoke a known-script skill on an empty directory and see if claude runs the script's steps or improvises.\n\n**Outcome → fix shape**:\n- If claude *does* resolve slash commands in `-p`: the asymmetry is real and structural. Fix shapes: (a) wire codex similarly — use codex's `codex exec review` subcommand and figure out how to layer scope/context (file-level config? env var? prepend as system prompt?), or (b) replace the 28-word paraphrase with a much more scripted instruction set so codex's improvisation budget is bounded.\n- If claude does *not* resolve slash commands either: the asymmetry isn't workflow-level. Move on to Section 2's other hypotheses (model, reas