{"parentUuid":"7571960c-d7f3-430b-841c-d8f090e1c324","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01D2sFcQDkNdGVomecW1dHLv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\nThe smoking gun is sitting in claude's own help text. The `--bare` flag description explicitly says **\"Skills still resolve via /skill-name\"**, and there's a separate `--disable-slash-commands` flag whose description is literally **\"Disable all skills\"**. That's documentation-level confirmation that `claude -p`'s default behavior is to resolve `/skill-name` into a skill load. No runtime experiment needed.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n**Verified:** `claude -p <prompt>` **does** resolve `/skill-name` as a skill load by default (the CLI help calls this out as a noteworthy behavior of `--bare`). So when `entire review --agent claude-code` sends a prompt whose first lines are `/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\\n/silent-failure-hunter\\n...`, claude loads each skill file from `~/.claude/plugins/cache/...` and follows its scripted instructions.\n\nMeanwhile, codex is being handed a 28-word paraphrase. **The workflow asymmetry is real, structural, and explains a substantial chunk of the 2-5x gap** — independent of any model/sandbox/MCP timing differences.\n\nNow I can compress the rest of the design. Sections 2-4 below.\n\n## Section 2: Confirmed cause + remaining hypotheses (ranked)\n\n**Confirmed (no further investigation needed)**:\n\n1. **Codex never runs its native `/review`.** `expandCodexBuiltinReview` paraphrases the only built-in into 28 generic words before piping to `codex exec --json -`. Claude, by contrast, resolves `/pr-review-toolkit:...` etc. into actual skill executions via `claude -p`. **This alone is sufficient to produce 2-5x.**\n\n**Remaining (still hypotheses — to be confirmed/refuted by Approach B instrumentation, if the residual gap after the workflow fix is still meaningful)**:\n\n2. **Codex `exec` non-interactive defaults**: model, reasoning effort, sandbox mode may differ from the TUI's defaults. Verifiable via `codex config show` + log output.\n3. **Per-spawn cold-start cost**: MCP servers, lifecycle hooks (codex `hooks.json`), seatbelt sandbox warmup, auth/model-cache fetch. Verifiable via `time codex exec --json - <<< \"\"` baseline vs. TUI startup.\n4. **Codex's `DiscoverReviewSkills` is stubbed** → no codex-side plugin skills can be picked → codex workflow can never be as scripted as claude's. Affects long-term parity even if (1) is fixed.\n5. **Gemini's `gemini -p \" \"` invocation**: uncharacterized. Same paraphrase risk (does gemini have a `/review` equivalent that's being paraphrased?), same `-p` mode question (does gemini resolve `/...` skills?), same buffering question (pre-#1192 carry-over).\n\n## Section 3: Fix shapes per cause\n\nFor (1) — the confirmed workflow asymmetry — there are three viable fix shapes, each with tradeoffs:\n\n- **3a. Switch to `codex exec review --json --base <ref>`.** Use codex's native review subcommand. Lose the ability to layer scope/per-run prompt/checkpoint context directly into the prompt. Mitigation: inject context via the codex hook's `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` mechanism (per `codex/AGENT.md`), or via env vars the hook reads.\n- **3b. Replace the 28-word paraphrase with a much more scripted instruction set.** Keep using `codex exec -`, but make the prompt look like a real review-skill script (steps, files to check, output format). Doesn't capture any codex-internal optimizations the native `/review` might have, but is fully under our control.\n- **3c. Implement codex's `DiscoverReviewSkills`** + ship a codex-side review skill plugin. Long-term parity with claude's plugin model. Bigger scope; not for this PR.\n\n**Recommended:** **3a as primary, 3b as fallback** when the hook-injection path can't carry the needed context. 3c is its own follow-up project that this spec should reference but not block on.\n\nFor (2)/(3) — `exec`-mode defaults and cold-start — add per-event timing instrumentation to the review pipeline (already enabled by PR #1192's streaming surface). Run controlled head-to-head experiments **after** (3a) lands, to measure whether residual gap exists.\n\nFor (5) — gemini — apply the same diff: does gemini `-p \" \"` resolve `/...` as skills? does gemini have a native `/review`? Same fix tree.\n\n## Section 4: Experimental protocol for the residual gap (Approach B)\n\nOnly relevant if Section 3 fixes don't fully close the gap. Sketch:\n\n1. Add `time.Now()` per `Event` to a debug log behind `ENTIRE_REVIEW_TIMING=1` (or similar). Cheap; no UX impact when off.\n2. Run 3 invocations each of: `entire review --agent claude-code`, `entire review --agent codex` (post-3a fix), `entire review --agent gemini-cli`, against the same branch and scope.\n3. Compute per-agent: time-to-first-event, mean inter-event interval, total wall-clock, total tool-call count, total tokens (where available).\n4. Decision tree:\n   - If codex's mean inter-event is high and tool calls are few → reasoning effort / model differs. Try `codex --config reasoning_effort=low` or model override.\n   - If codex's tool-call count is high → codex is exploring more than claude (skill scripting still less tight than claude's). Refine 3a/3b prompt.\n   - If time-to-first-event is high but inter-event is normal → cold-start dominates. Investigate MCP/hooks/sandbox warmup.\n\nDoes this compressed design (Sections 2-4) look right? If yes, I'll write the spec doc, commit it, and run the spec-review loop. Anything you want to add, remove, or reorder?"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3894,"cache_read_input_tokens":131723,"output_tokens":3967,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_t